        },
        {
          "post_id": "533262",
          "text": "<p data-start=\"225\" data-end=\"403\">Let $(v_n)$ be a sequence defined by $v_1=2$ and $v_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(v_n+\\frac{8}{v_n}\\right)$ for $n\\geq1$. If $(v_n)$ converges, then $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}v_n$ is:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"455\">$2$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"455\">$2\\sqrt2$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"455\">$4$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"455\">non-existent</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "B",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "subject": "Calculus",
          "day": "Day 283"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "533257",
          "text": "<p data-start=\"30\" data-end=\"143\">The point(s) of inflection of the curve $$y=\\frac{2}{5}x^6-\\frac{9}{5}x^5-3x^4+22x^3-36x^2+5x+1$$ is/are:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"226\">$x=-2,1,3$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"226\">$x=-2$ and $x=3$ only<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"226\">$x=1$ only<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"226\">None of the above</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "B",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "subject": "Calculus",
          "day": "Day 283"
        },
        {
          "post_id": "533252",
          "text": "<p data-start=\"40\" data-end=\"171\">At the point $x=1$, the function $f(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-1)^2\\sin\\left(\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right),&x\\neq1\\\\\\\\0,&x=1\\end{cases}$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; is:</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"338\">not continuous<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"338\">continuous but not differentiable<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"338\">differentiable, but $f'$ is not continuous at $x=1$<br>&nbsp;</li><li data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"338\">differentiable and $f'$ is continuous at $x=1$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
